<section class="section" aria-labelledby="first-title">
  <div class="wrap">
    <h2 id="first-title" class="plain-h2">Your first hour</h2>
    <ol class="decide">
      <li><h3>Find the reply date</h3><p>It is the most important thing on the page. Note it in your calendar, and treat the date on the notice as the one that counts. Missing it can lead to an order being passed without your side being heard.</p></li>
      <li><h3>Check that it is real</h3><p>Look for it on the {% ext "https://www.gst.gov.in/", "GST portal" %}, under your login, and check your own registration details. Be careful of emails or calls asking for payment or for OTPs.</p></li>
      <li><h3>Identify the form and the period</h3><p>Note the form number, the section, the tax period and the amount demanded.</p></li>
      <li><h3>Stop anything that could make it worse</h3><p>Do not alter old records. Keep everything together. Tell your accountant, and ask who has been reading portal notices.</p></li>
      <li><h3>Decide who will handle it</h3><p>A simple mismatch may be different from a demand or fraud allegation. Get qualified advice before you reply to anything serious.</p></li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section--tint" aria-labelledby="types-title">
  <div class="wrap">
    <header class="section-head">
      <h2 id="types-title">Common notices</h2>
      <p>As described by secondary guides. Forms and sections change, so rely on what your notice says.</p>
    </header>
    <section class="data-table-wrap" tabindex="0" aria-label="Notice types, scrolls sideways on small screens">
      <table class="data-table">
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Form</th><th scope="col">Generally means</th><th scope="col">Usual first response</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th scope="row">ASMT-10</th><td>Scrutiny found discrepancies in your returns</td><td>Explain or correct. Reply is reported to be in ASMT-11</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">DRC-01A</th><td>An intimation before a formal show cause notice</td><td>Review carefully. Paying or replying at this stage can matter</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">DRC-01</th><td>A show cause notice demanding tax. From FY 2024-25, reported as under section 74A</td><td>Reply in DRC-06. Commonly cited as within 30 days, while some sources cite a longer period under section 74A. Check your notice</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">REG-17</th><td>A notice to show why your registration should not be cancelled</td><td>Respond quickly, as cancellation affects your whole business</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">DRC-07</th><td>An order or summary of demand</td><td>Consider appeal rights immediately. Reported as three months under section 107, with a pre-deposit</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </section>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section" aria-labelledby="who-title">
  <div class="wrap layout-side">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2 id="who-title" class="first-h2">Who can do what</h2>
      <section class="data-table-wrap" tabindex="0" aria-label="Who can do what on a notice, scrolls sideways on small screens">
        <table class="data-table">
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Task</th><th scope="col">Who</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row">Reconciling your records for the notice</th><td>Your accountant can often do this</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Drafting a reply</th><td>Someone who understands GST law. Ask about similar cases</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Appearing for you before an officer</th><td>An authorised representative under section 116, such as your relative or regular employee, an advocate, a practising CA, cost accountant or company secretary, or certain others, including a GST practitioner authorised by you, subject to its conditions</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Appeals and litigation</th><td>Usually an advocate or experienced practitioner. Court proceedings usually involve an advocate</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </section>
      <p>Not every accountant handles disputes, and that is no criticism of them. Rule 84 says no one may attend as a GST practitioner unless enrolled under rule 83, and they may be asked to show your authorisation in Form GST PCT-05. Ask directly whether they do this work, and in which capacity.</p>

      <h2 id="gather">What to gather</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>The notice and all earlier correspondence</li>
        <li>Returns for the period (GSTR-1, GSTR-3B, annual return if any)</li>
        <li>Your sales and purchase registers, and invoices for the items queried</li>
        <li>Your GSTR-2B, and your reconciliation of it</li>
        <li>Payment challans and ledger statements</li>
        <li>Any explanations, such as a supplier cancelled or a credit note issued</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="avoid">What to avoid</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Replying with a generic letter that does not address the specific points</li>
        <li>Paying without understanding whether you owe it, and without checking the correct form for payment</li>
        <li>Letting someone reply in your login without showing you</li>
        <li>Assuming that an agent can make it go away. No one can promise an outcome</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="role">Where AccountOrbit fits</h2>
      <p>AccountOrbit does not review notices or give legal advice, and it cannot promise that anyone will be available in time. If your reply date is close, do not wait on us.</p>

